 ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 :stop-rules
 {:improvement-programme :none
  :rationale
  "Deliberate. A commit freeze to collect N comparable runs was proposed by
   the Fable F27 review and withdrawn: repairs are needed to complete a run at
   all (Joe). Continuous repair is a standing condition of this experiment, not
   a defect in it. A stopping rule over a drifting instrument would protect
   nothing.
   In a continuously repaired system the live threat is NOT optional stopping.
   It is SELECTIVE EXCLUSION -- 'that frame does not count, the harness was
   broken'. Both F27 reviews establish that such frames will occur (e.g.
   b95dff66: the solver proceeded silently without its role card -- 'this does
   not stall the run, it degrades the evidence'). The total-reporting rule
   below is what stands in place of a stop rule."
  :ablation-trigger
  {:precondition
   "The ablation is only worth running once the memory arm has actually closed
    something. Ablating against a memory arm that also fails compares dead with
    deader (Joe, 2026-08-24) -- two nulls, no signal about transfer."
   :on-set "ALL F29+ frames in which the student solved with memory offered."
   :note-on-set
   "Not a guard against anyone's judgement -- the set follows from the
    precondition. Stating it now simply means the ablation set is fixed by a
    rule rather than settled after the frames are read."
   :report-with
   "The instrument-version distribution of the ablated set. Because transfer is
    being actively improved, the solved-with-memory frames will skew late, so
    the ablation speaks about the CURRENT transfer implementation and not about
    an F29-onward average. Reporting the distribution is what keeps that from
    being read as more general than it is."

   :power-band
   {:observation
    "The ablation has power only on problems in a band: hard enough that the
     student fails alone, tractable enough that memories lift it. Outside the
     band there is no signal in either direction -- too easy and both arms
     solve, too hard and both arms fail."
    :consequence-for-corpus
    "Problem difficulty therefore has to be SELECTED for the band, not
     inherited from whatever the pool supplies. This is a live constraint: the
     held-out problems are currently set aside as 'too hard' (Joe), which is
     the correct call for level-2b and is exactly what makes them the right
     instrument for level-3."
    :consequence-for-nulls
    "A both-arms-fail frame is not wasted. It is uninformative about TRANSFER
     but informative about PROBLEM SELECTION -- evidence the problem sits above
     the band. Under total reporting it is reported as such rather than
     discarded, and it is how the band gets located empirically."
    :relation-to-levels
    "Level-2b requires problems INSIDE the band; level-3 deliberately uses
     problems ABOVE it. That difficulty gap is precisely the functioning /
     capability distinction made operational."}}}

