  {:amendment 8
   :at "2026-08-26T18:30Z"
   :by "claude-clink-1"
   :numbering-note
   "Written as amendment 5 and renumbered to 8. Amendments 5, 6 and 7 were
    landed by claude-19 between 16:20 and 18:20 while this one was being
    drafted; the file's tail read as amendment 4 when it was opened. Recorded
    rather than silently corrected, because a registration whose numbering was
    once ambiguous should say so."
   :authority "Joe, 2026-08-26, in session"
   :kind :protocol-revision
   :change
   "Student attempt 1 is handed a shelf with every SAME-PROBLEM memory withheld.
    A memory whose :evidence/subject problem-id equals the frame's problem is
    filtered out of the attempt-1 snapshot. Attempts 2 and 3 are unchanged."

   :why
   "Tier-A condition 3 -- transfer ACROSS problems, through the shelf -- has
    n=1 across the whole campaign (f42/a1: mined by f29-guide on a01J05, used
    on a97J07, fingerprinted, surfaced at position 47 of 48). Everything else
    measured is within-frame: f42/a3 used two memories, both f42-scribe on
    a97J07; f44/a1 used three, all f44-scribe on a98A02. Amendment 3 already
    rules that within-frame attempts are not a contrast and must not be
    reported as one, so those uses are apparatus signal and contribute nothing
    to the question the campaign exists to answer.

    Withholding same-problem memories on one attempt makes every use on that
    attempt cross-problem BY CONSTRUCTION. Condition 3 stops depending on a
    student happening to reach past its own frame's deposits."

   :why-attempt-1
   "Attempt 1 is the cleanest read: it is the only attempt whose shelf is not
    also contaminated by deposits mined from EARLIER attempts of the same
    frame. Note that same-problem memories are present even at attempt 1 --
    promote-solver runs before the student lane, so the scribe has already
    mined the Solver's work -- so this is a real holdout at a1, not a no-op."

   :relation-to-the-ablation-arm
   "This is NOT the :ablation arm. That arm is {:memory :withheld} entirely,
    status :not-yet-run, and amendment 4 records that it has never existed.
    This is a partial, targeted holdout: memory is still offered, only the
    same-problem cache is removed. It tests whether transfer happens when the
    cache is absent, which is the open question; full withholding tests whether
    memory helps at all, which the campaign has already answered in the
    affirmative."

   :confound/unresolved
   "Withholding same-problem memories shrinks the shelf AND changes its
    composition. If attempt 1 fares worse, that is consistent with both 'cross-
    problem memory does not transfer' and 'fewer memories'. A size-matched
    control -- withholding an equal count of randomly chosen CROSS-problem
    memories on another attempt -- would separate them. Not adopted here; Joe
    has not ruled on it. Until it is, the attempt-1 contrast is suggestive
    about transfer and not clean, and reports must say so."

   :comparability
   "Frames from this amendment forward are NOT comparable to f28-f44 on
    attempt-1 outcomes. The independent variable changed. Any tally spanning
    the boundary must segment at it."

   :implementation
   "One predicate at snapshot assembly, where candidate-visible? already runs:
    drop candidates whose provenance problem-id equals the frame's. Not yet
    built at the time of writing."}

